Semaphore
---------

* It is a small positive integer which is present in a kernel space and which is used to synchronize between multiple process when they are accessing shared resource. 

	1. semget()
	2. semctl()
	3. semop()

	semget() is useful for creating as well as opening the semaphore set.
	
	semctl() is useful for setting the semaphore value and getting the semaphore value and doing some other control operation on the semaphore set.
	
	semop() is desired the process should enter into the critical section or not. It is also blocking function.



* In the place of semaphore, fcntl  not used.
* In the place of fcntl, semaphore is used.


semget()
--------

* get a semaphore identifier

	int semget(key_t key, int nsems, int semflg);

using the semget() function, we can create the array of semaphore (array of integer).

	
	ipcs -s :- using this we can get currently how many semaphore set.


semctl()
--------

* semaphore control operations

	int semctl(int semid, int semnum, int cmd, ...);

	GETVAL -> getting the value
	SETVAL -> setting the value

	by default when we create the semaphore set in kernel space all are zeros.


semop()
-------

* semaphore operations

	int semop(int semid, struct sembuf* sops, unsigned nsops);

	struct sembuf {

		unsigned short sem_num;	// semaphore number
		short sem_op;	// semaphore operation
		short sem_flg;	// operation flags
	};

	* sem_op decide process should enter or not enter the critical section.


main() {

	struct sembuf v;
	int id, ret;
	
	id = semget(5, 5, IPC_CREAT | 0644);
	
	if(id < 0) {

		perror("Semget");
		return;
	}
	
	printf("id = %d\n", id);

	v.sem_num = 2;
	v.sem_op =  		// +ve, -ve or 0
	v.sem_flg = 0;

	printf("Before...\n");
	semop(id, &v, 1);
	printf("After...\n");
}

1. If sem_op is zero, the process must have read permission on the semaphore set. This is a "wait for zero" operation. If semval is zero the operation can immediately proceed.

2. If sem_op is +ve integer, the operation add this value to the semaphore value (semval). The operation can always proceed. It never forces a process to wait.
	
	v.sem_num = 2;
	v.sem_op = 2;
	v.sem_flg = SEM_UNDO;
	
	printf("Before ...\n");
	semop(id, &v, 1);
	sleep(10);
	
	+ve means always enter in a critical section.
	
3. If sem_op is less than zero, the process must have after permission on the semaphore set. If semval is greater than or equal to the absolute value of sem_op, the operation can proceed immediately the absolute value of the sem_op is substracted from sem_val.

	* sem_val >= |sem_op|
	   
		1 >= |-2|

		then block

	* sem_val >= |sem_op|

		4 >= |-2|
		
		Enter in critical section

	v.sem_num = 2;
	v.sem_op = -2;
	v.sem_flg = 0;

	printf("Before...\n");
	semop(id, &v, 1);
	sleep(1);
	printf("done\n");
	
	if sem_val is 1, block
	if sem_val is 2, enter in critical section
	if sem_val is 3, also enter in critical section

	


Semaphore are 2 types
---------------------

1. Binary semaphore (only 0 & 1)
2. Counting semaphore (all positive value allowed)

We can use counting semaphore as a binary semaphore
Binary semaphore can not use as a counting semaphore



Deadlock
--------

deadlock is a situation in which two or more processes are unable to proceed because each is waiting for the other to release resources or take an action.

 




	
















